EXAMPLES = [
("what's the difference between ram and storage",
"""Storage is where your computer keeps things for the long term: your files, your apps, the operating system. It's large and it remembers everything when the power is off, but it's comparatively slow. RAM is the computer's working space. When you open a program or a document, it gets copied from storage into RAM, where the processor can reach it almost instantly. RAM is much faster but much smaller, and it forgets everything when the computer shuts down. A good analogy is a desk and a filing cabinet: the cabinet holds everything you own, and the desk holds what you're working on right now. More RAM means a bigger desk, so you can keep more open at once without constantly fetching things from the cabinet."""),

("is it too late to learn to code at 35",
"""Not at all. Plenty of people start programming in their thirties, forties, and beyond, and they bring advantages that younger learners often lack: discipline, a clearer sense of why they're learning, and experience in some other field, which can be enormously valuable when paired with technical skills. What matters far more than your age is consistency. An hour a day for a year will take you further than an intense month followed by burnout. Pick one language, build small projects you care about, and expect the first few months to feel slow. They feel slow for everyone."""),

("can you explain recursion",
"""Recursion is when a function solves a problem by calling itself on a smaller version of the same problem. Imagine you're standing in a long line and want to know your position, but you can only talk to the person in front of you. You ask them their position and plan to add one. They do the same thing, asking the person in front of them, and so on, until the question reaches the first person in line, who simply knows the answer: one. Then the answers travel back down the line, each person adding one, until you get yours.

Every recursive function needs those two parts: a base case, like the first person in line, that can be answered directly, and a recursive step that moves the problem closer to that base case. Without a base case, the function would keep calling itself forever, or at least until the computer runs out of memory."""),

("what is an api",
"""An API, or application programming interface, is a defined way for one piece of software to ask another for something. Think of a restaurant: you don't walk into the kitchen and cook your own meal; you read the menu, place an order with a waiter, and the kitchen sends back your food. The menu is the API. It lists what you can ask for and how to ask for it, while hiding everything that happens in the kitchen. When a weather app shows you the forecast, it's usually sending a request to a weather service's API and displaying the data that comes back."""),

("why is my computer so slow",
"""The most common causes are surprisingly ordinary. Too many programs running at once, especially browsers with dozens of tabs, can fill up your memory, forcing the computer to shuffle data back and forth to storage. A nearly full hard drive slows things down too. Programs that launch themselves at startup and run quietly in the background add up over time. And older computers with spinning hard drives rather than solid-state drives are much slower to load anything. A good first step is to open your task manager, called Activity Monitor on a Mac, and see what's using the most memory and processor time. Restarting occasionally also clears out a surprising amount of accumulated clutter."""),

("what's the difference between a junior and senior engineer",
"""The biggest difference usually isn't technical skill, though that matters; it's scope and judgment. A junior engineer is typically given well-defined tasks and needs guidance on how to approach them. A senior engineer can take an ambiguous problem, figure out what actually needs to be built, anticipate where things will go wrong, and make reasonable trade-offs without being told. Senior engineers also multiply the work of others, through code reviews, design discussions, mentoring, and making good decisions that keep the team from wasting time. A useful shorthand: junior engineers answer questions, and senior engineers figure out which questions are worth asking."""),

("how do i write a good bug report",
"""Write it so that someone who has never seen the problem can reproduce it on the first try. Include a short, specific title, like "Checkout button does nothing when the cart contains a gift card," rather than "Checkout broken." Then list the exact steps to trigger the bug, what you expected to happen, and what actually happened. Add details about your environment, such as the browser, device, app version, and account type, along with any error messages, screenshots, or logs. If you've noticed when it does and doesn't happen, say so; that kind of narrowing down can save hours. A good bug report is a gift to the person who has to fix it."""),

("what is technical debt",
"""Technical debt is the future cost of taking shortcuts in software today. When a team ships something quickly by skipping tests, copying code instead of organizing it properly, or putting off a needed redesign, they gain speed now but create work later, and like financial debt, it accumulates interest: each new feature built on top of the shortcut is harder to make and more fragile. Some technical debt is a perfectly reasonable choice, just as a mortgage can be. The trouble comes when nobody tracks it, and the interest quietly grows until every change takes three times as long as it should."""),

("what makes a good code review comment",
"""A good comment is specific, explains its reasoning, and is clear about how much it matters. "This could cause a null pointer error if the user hasn't set a profile picture; maybe check for that here?" is far more useful than "this looks wrong." It also helps to separate blocking issues from suggestions, perhaps by marking minor preferences with a label like "nit" or "optional," so the author knows what must change and what's merely a thought. Ask questions when you're unsure rather than assuming a mistake. And remember to comment on things done well; a review that only lists problems teaches people to dread reviews."""),

("explain git to a beginner",
"""Git is a tool that keeps a detailed history of changes to a project, so you can see what changed, when, and why, and go back if something breaks. Imagine writing an essay where every time you reach a good stopping point, you take a snapshot of the whole document with a short note describing what you changed. Those snapshots are called commits. Git also lets you create branches, separate lines of work where you can experiment without affecting the main version, and later merge your changes back in if they work out. Services like GitHub store a copy of the project online so that a team can share it, review each other's changes, and work in parallel without overwriting one another."""),

("how do i run a good meeting",
"""Start by asking whether it needs to be a meeting at all; many can be an email. If it does, write down its purpose in one sentence and share an agenda in advance, so people arrive prepared. Invite only those who are needed to make a decision or contribute. Start on time, keep the discussion on the agenda, and gently park tangents for later. Before you end, summarize what was decided, who will do what, and by when, and send those notes out afterward. The best meetings end early, with everyone knowing exactly what happens next."""),

("how do i get better at public speaking",
"""Practice out loud, as often as you can, in front of real people. Reading your notes silently teaches you almost nothing about how the words will feel in your mouth. Know your opening and your closing cold; those are where nerves strike hardest and where audiences form their impressions. Slow down, much more than feels natural, because nervous speakers almost always rush. Pause instead of filling silences with "um"; a pause sounds confident from the audience's side. And look for low-stakes chances to speak, like a short update at a team meeting or a local group such as Toastmasters. Comfort comes from repetition, not from talent."""),

("what is the cloud",
""""The cloud" is a friendly name for other people's computers, enormous numbers of them, housed in data centers around the world and rented out over the internet. When you store photos in the cloud, they're saved on servers owned by a company like Apple, Google, or Amazon rather than on your own device. When a business runs its software in the cloud, it's renting computing power instead of buying and maintaining its own machines. The appeal is flexibility: you can use as much or as little as you need, access it from anywhere, and let someone else worry about the hardware."""),

("whats the difference between http and https",
"""Both are the rules your browser uses to request web pages, but HTTPS adds encryption. With plain HTTP, the information traveling between your browser and the website, including anything you type into a form, could in principle be read or altered by anyone positioned along the way, such as someone on the same public Wi-Fi network. HTTPS scrambles that traffic so that only your browser and the website can read it, and it uses certificates to help confirm that you're really talking to the site you think you are. The padlock in the address bar is your sign that a connection is using HTTPS."""),

("how do i explain my job as a software engineer to my grandma",
"""Start with something she uses. You might say: "You know how your phone shows you the weather, or how the bank lets you check your balance on the computer? Somebody has to write the instructions that make those things work, step by step, in a language the computer understands. That's what I do. I write the instructions, and then I spend a lot of time figuring out why the computer didn't follow them the way I expected." If she asks more, describe one specific thing you built and who uses it. People connect with the purpose of a job far more than with its tools."""),

("how do i stay focused working from home",
"""Create boundaries that your surroundings used to provide for you. Work in a consistent place, ideally not your bed, and start and finish at roughly the same times each day, with a small ritual to mark the beginning and end, like a short walk around the block. Plan your day the night before, with one or two priorities rather than a long list. Keep your phone out of reach during focused stretches, and work in blocks of forty-five or ninety minutes with real breaks in between. And get out of the house at least once a day; the days blur together quickly when they all happen in the same three rooms."""),

("what's a good way to learn sql",
"""Learn it by asking real questions of real data. Start with the basics, selecting columns, filtering with WHERE, sorting, and counting, using a free interactive tutorial that lets you type queries in the browser. Then download a dataset you find genuinely interesting, such as your city's public data, sports statistics, or your own exported spending history, and load it into a simple database like SQLite. Ask it questions you actually want answered: which month did I spend the most? Which team scores more at home? Joins and grouping, which trip up most learners, make sense much faster when you're using them to answer something you care about."""),

("what is a database index",
"""An index is a separate structure that a database keeps alongside a table to help it find rows quickly, much like the index at the back of a book. Without one, finding every customer named "Garcia" means reading every row in the table, one by one. With an index on the name column, the database keeps those names in sorted order, with pointers to where each row lives, so it can jump almost straight to the right place. The trade-off is that every index takes up space and must be updated whenever data changes, so writes get slightly slower. You add indexes on the columns you search or sort by often, not on everything."""),

("should i specialize or be a generalist",
"""Early in a career, breadth tends to pay off: you learn how different parts of a system fit together, discover what you enjoy, and become adaptable. Over time, most people benefit from developing real depth in one or two areas, because deep expertise is rarer and harder to replace. A common and effective shape is to be broadly competent across your field and genuinely excellent in one corner of it. Choose that corner by what you find yourself curious about even when nobody's asking, since that curiosity is what sustains the long, unglamorous work of becoming an expert."""),

("how do i write a good design doc",
"""Write it to help people make a decision, not to prove you've thought of everything. Open with the problem and why it matters now, in a paragraph a busy reader can absorb in a minute. State the goals, and just as important, the non-goals, which keep the discussion from sprawling. Describe your proposed approach in enough detail that a reviewer can find its weaknesses, and then describe the alternatives you considered and why you rejected them; that section is often the most valuable one. End with the risks and the open questions you'd like input on. A design doc that invites disagreement early saves a great deal of disagreement later."""),

("what is a race condition",
"""A race condition happens when the correctness of a program depends on the timing of things that run at the same time. Imagine two people sharing a bank account, each at a different ATM, each withdrawing a hundred dollars from a balance of a hundred and fifty. Each machine checks the balance, sees a hundred and fifty, approves the withdrawal, and subtracts a hundred. If both checks happen before either subtraction, both withdrawals succeed, and the bank has handed out two hundred dollars from an account that held a hundred and fifty. Each step was correct on its own; the bug lives in the gap between checking and acting. The usual fix is to make that check-and-update happen as a single, indivisible operation, often with a lock, so that only one of them can be in the middle of it at a time."""),

("how should i prepare for a system design interview",
"""Practice designing systems out loud, under time pressure, because the interview tests how you reason through ambiguity more than what you've memorized. Start every problem by asking clarifying questions: who uses this, how many of them, what matters most, speed, cost, or consistency? Then sketch a simple design that works before adding complexity, and explain your trade-offs as you go. Learn the common building blocks, such as load balancers, caches, queues, databases, and replication, well enough to explain why you'd reach for each one. And practice with another person if you can; talking through a design while someone pushes back is very different from reading about one."""),

("explain dns simply",
"""DNS, the Domain Name System, is the internet's phone book. Computers find each other using numerical addresses, like 93.184.216.34, but people prefer names like example.com. When you type a name into your browser, your computer asks a DNS server what number belongs to that name. If that server doesn't know, it asks others, working down from the servers responsible for ".com" to the ones responsible for that specific domain, until it gets an answer. The result is cached along the way, so the next lookup is faster. All of this usually takes a few milliseconds, before the page even begins to load."""),

("what's the difference between concurrency and parallelism",
"""Concurrency is about dealing with many things at once; parallelism is about doing many things at once. Picture a single cook preparing a meal: she starts the rice, chops vegetables while it simmers, checks the oven, and returns to the rice, juggling several tasks by switching between them. That's concurrency. Now picture three cooks, each handling one dish at the same moment. That's parallelism. A program can be concurrent on a single processor core by interleaving tasks, and it becomes parallel when multiple cores truly run tasks simultaneously. Concurrency is a way of structuring work; parallelism is a way of executing it."""),

("how do i ask a senior engineer for help without being annoying",
"""Show that you've already tried. Before you ask, spend a reasonable amount of time on the problem, perhaps thirty minutes to an hour, depending on how urgent it is. Then write a short message that explains what you're trying to do, what you expected, what's happening instead, and what you've already ruled out. That makes it easy for them to help quickly, and it often helps you find the answer yourself while you're writing it. Ask at a natural break, or in a channel they can answer when convenient, rather than interrupting deep work. Most senior engineers are glad to help someone who asks this way; what wears them down is being asked to start from zero."""),
]
